@startuml failure-handling-flow
title Choosing a failure mechanism

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam activityDiamondFontSize 11
skinparam noteFontSize 11

start

:A call can fail;

if (Is this a normal, frequent outcome?\ncache miss, empty queue, not ready) then (yes)
  :Return std::optional\nor a status enum;
  stop
endif

if (Is the program wrong?\nimpossible state, broken invariant) then (yes)
  :VISCERA_ASSERT in Debug and RelWithDebInfo,\nor VISCERA_PANIC in every config;
  :panic() writes a 1024-byte stack buffer,\nwrites stderr, and aborts;
  note right
    Does not call the logger.
    Does not flush.
    Does not unwind.
  end note
  stop
endif

if (Did allocation fail,\nor can an Error not be built?) then (yes)
  :panic() as above;
  stop
endif

:Return a Result failure.\nNew errors carry a code, a static description, and an origin.\nwithContext or wrap only when this layer has something to add.\nbad_alloc while attaching a frame calls panic().\nDo not log.;

if (Can this layer decide the outcome?\nrecover, show the user, discard, or stop the process) then (yes)
  if (Can the process continue?) then (yes)
    :Log once.\nHandler chooses level and category.\nRecover, or log Debug if discarding.;
    stop
  else (no)
    :This is main, or the same top-level handler.\nLog once at Fatal.\nflush, shut down in order,\nreturn non-zero.;
    note right
      Fatal does not abort.
      Subsystems do not call panic()
      for an environment failure.
    end note
    stop
  endif
else (no)
  :return std::unexpected(std::move(result).error())\nwith optional withContext or wrap.\nDo not log.;
  stop
endif

@enduml
